# Build context is the repository root (infra/docker/README.md). Keep it minimal.

# --- secrets: never in a layer ---
.env
.env.*
**/*.csv
**/*secret*
**/*credential*
**/*_api_keys*
**/service-account*.json
**/*.pem
**/*.key

# --- version control, CI, docs ---
.git
.github
docs
*.md
PROJECT_SPECIFICATION.md
fixtures
scripts
infra/terraform
infra/kubernetes
infra/sql

# --- python ---
.venv
**/__pycache__
**/*.py[cod]
.mypy_cache
.pytest_cache
.ruff_cache
.hypothesis
htmlcov
.coverage
packages/*/tests

# --- node ---
# The front end was deleted on 2026-09-09; these patterns are kept because they name
# build output rather than a particular app, and a Node surface that returns would
# otherwise ship its `.next` directory into an image on the first day.
#
# `.next` was the only build directory named here, and the front end that returned does
# not use it. `vinext` writes `.vinext/`, so the developer's own cache was inside the
# build context, `COPY apps/razorsharp-concept/ ./` carried it into the image, and the
# build layered on top of it. The result shipped and was worse than a stale asset: the
# bundle referenced fonts by their absolute path on the machine that had last built
# locally -- `/Users/<name>/.../.vinext/fonts/...`, a 404 for every visitor and a
# developer's directory tree published on a public site -- and the chunk graph it
# produced was incoherent enough that `loadNavigationModule()` resolved to the wrong
# module. Every client-side link on the deployed site did nothing when clicked.
#
# `dist` is the same hazard one step later: 23 MB of previous output sitting in the
# context, copied in, and built over. Both are regenerated by `npm run build` inside the
# image, so neither has any reason to cross the boundary.
**/node_modules
**/.next
**/.vinext
**/dist
**/out
**/coverage
**/*.tsbuildinfo
**/.env.local

# --- os / editor / logs ---
.DS_Store
.idea
.vscode
**/*.log
